# The universe hierarchy, the spine of the type theory. Types are themselves values, so they need types, and a single
# self-typed universe (a type of all types, including itself) is inconsistent by Girard's paradox. The fix is a tower:
# Type 0, Type 1, Type 2, and on, each universe a member of the next. The hierarchy is predicative (a universe is built
# only from universes below it), cumulative (a type in a lower universe also sits in every higher one, so a small type
# is usable where a larger one is expected), and universe-polymorphic (a definition is written once over a level
# variable and instantiates at every level). The one exception is the bottom: Type 0 is impredicative, so a function
# landing in Type 0 stays in Type 0 whatever its domain, which is what lets a self-encoded inductive type live in the
# bottom universe instead of floating above its own induction motive. This is the impredicative-bottom discipline, not
# a self-typed universe.
#
# The rules: Type i has type Type (i + 1). A function type inhabits the maximum of its domain's and codomain's
# universe, except when the codomain is the bottom, where the function type stays in the bottom. Comparison of levels
# is decidable, which is what makes universe polymorphism mechanical.

# A level: the address of a universe. A constant floor joined with the maximum over level variables, each carried with
# a non-negative offset. The floor alone names a concrete universe (Type 0, Type 1, and on); the variables make a
# definition polymorphic over levels.
form level
  link floor, like natural
  link variables, like text

# The universe at a level: the type whose members are exactly the types of that level. Type i is a member of Type
# (i + 1), and by cumulativity also of every universe above.
form universe
  link level, like level
